      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "[Literature audit 2026-11-05 (deliverome-analysis PR 236), verdict confirmed_shifted: insert_after_residue 34 -> 33 on human canonical Q8N131 (paper numbering: human TMEM123 (Q8N131) canonical numbering; endogenous CRISPR knock-in in U2OS (human) cells, construct species: human). Paper (PMID ): \"we added the Alfa tag to the N-terminal region following the signal peptide (Alanine 34)\". Audit note: Secondary source: the genome-editing oligo table in the Methods of bioRxiv 10.1101/2025.06.08.658482v1 (full-text PDF; the table is not in the HTML). The row is labelled 'TMEM123 / A33' (not A34) and the ssODN CTGGGGACGCTGCAGGTGCTAGCGCTGCTGGGGGCCGCCCATGAAAGCGCGGCTATGGCG-GGCAGC-[Alfa CCCAGCAGG...GAGCCC]-GGCAGC-G|GTAAGTGG... translates to LGTLQVLALLGAAHESAAMA (human residues 14-33) + GS + PSRLEEELRRRLTEP + GS, then the split codon G|CC of A34 at the exon 1 splice donor. So the tag (GS-Alfa-GS) sits between A33 and A34: insert_after 33, one residue before the row's 34; the text's 'Alanine 34' is the first residue after the insert. Species is human by construction (endogenous locus edited in U2OS; left homology arm matches the human CDS exactly). Surface evidence unchanged (live-cell anti-Alfa nanobody binding/uptake); no function assay.] The paper explicitly names 'Alanine 34' as the insertion point, and canonical residue 34 is indeed A (confirmed by reading the numbered sequence). The tag is placed after residue 34 (residue_before=A, residue_after=S at position 35), 8 residues into the extracellular domain and 135 residues from the TM boundary \u2014 well clear of all topology constraints. Because the paper names the exact residue and reports a surface-displayed construct, position_evidence is 'validated' and evidence_type is the exact-site value. Full-length TMEM123 expressed in mammalian cells with an ALFA tag inserted after Ala34, 8 residues into the extracellular domain (canonical residues 27\u2013168), which the authors describe as predicted unstructured. The construct was engineered to display the tag on the cell surface.",
